# KNOWN GAP repro — BOTH compilers OVER-REJECT this memory-safe program.
#
# `Tag.A` and `Tag.B` are every variant of `Tag` and both arms `return`, so no
# value of `g` can fall out of the `match` and `print(s)` is unreachable from
# the branch that moved `s`. Both move checkers classify fall-through
# SYNTACTICALLY — Rust on a literal `else_arm`, the self-host on
# `match_has_catch_all` — and neither consults an exhaustiveness verdict at the
# DIVERGENCE site, so both emit `E_UseAfterMove`.
#
# INTENDED: ACCEPTED by both lanes, printing
#   sunk
#   hello
#
# The fix is a SECOND verdict bit recording IRREFUTABLE coverage. The bare
# exhaustiveness verdict the typechecker computes is NOT that bit: it credits a
# REFUTABLE nested sub-pattern as full coverage, so fusing it in here would
# accept a genuine use-after-move (see `TODO.md`, the entry beginning
# "`collect_covered_variants` counts a REFUTABLE NESTED SUB-PATTERN", and the
# live NEG cell `tests/fixtures/liveness/
# match_nested_refutable_diverge_use_after_move_reject.gg` that catches it).
#
# Its JOIN-site sibling is `sh_exhaustive_match_not_credited_at_join.gg`. The
# current REJECT behaviour is pinned meanwhile by
# `tests/fixtures/liveness/match_exhaustive_no_catchall_diverge_reject.gg`,
# which MOVES to `self_host_driver_accepts_liveness` when this graduates.
enum Tag:
    A(int)
    B(int)

void sink(String ^x):
    print("sunk")

void probe(Tag g, bool c):
    String s = "hello"
    if c:
        sink(^s)
        match g:
            case Tag.A(_):
                return
            case Tag.B(_):
                return
    print(s)

void main():
    probe(Tag.A(1), true)
    probe(Tag.A(1), false)
